{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Cain was.</b> It would have been fitting to say 'and Cain was a tiller of the ground' (ויהי קין עובד אדמה), just as it says 'and Abel was' (והבל היה). And the reason he differentiated between them is that their nature was not equal, but they were two opposites — this one righteous and this one wicked. From here one may learn that good and evil have one root, all of it good: for the first man, Adam, was wholly good, and from him emerged one good and one evil. And from this premise we can grasp the signs of [divine] unity in the creation of man and his generations. And on this account Scripture says (Isaiah 45:7): ״עושה שלום ובורא רע אני ה׳ עושה כל אלה״ — 'I make peace and create evil; I am YHWH who does all these.' It needed to add כל אלה ('all these') lest a person entertain doubts about two governing powers when he sees diverse attributes in the world, for all of them are from one root, and from the mouth of the Most High come both evil and good. And this is what it says (ibid.): אני ה׳ — 'I am YHWH'; I am the One from whom all comes. And further, it needed to say וקין היה ('and Cain was'), to hint that he 'already was' — and the intent is upon his force and his cause, which existed already before man was created. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:03.458327145Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}